WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.355
Hi, I'm Neymar Aldanar, and this is Module 4 of OS

2
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:04.060
Work.

3
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.455
In Module 3, you opened the terminal and took your first

4
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.280
steps with codecs in a practice folder.

5
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.135
Now we're going to put the house in order around that

6
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:12.815
work.

7
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.055
In this module, you'll create a folder tree that keeps one

8
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.455
topic apart from another, write a readme in Markdown that explains

9
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.435
the project to whoever comes later, give each kind of note

10
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:29.255
its own file, and keep passwords and keys far away from

11
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.520
everything that gets shared.

12
00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.095
At the end, you write down what the AI should read

13
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:35.500
first.

14
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.855
Denise, a pedagogical coordinator, and Lucia, an 8th grade science

15
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.160
teacher, are still with us.

16
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.255
Every practice uses the same terminal from Module 3, with made-up

17
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.080
data.

18
00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.695
When I ask, pause the video and do it right then.

19
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.615
Think of the steel filing cabinet in the school office.

20
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.035
Each drawer holds one class, and nobody looks for 8th grade

21
00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.035
A in the 7th grade B drawer.

22
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.343
A work folder plays the same role.

23
00:01:01.368 --> 00:01:03.563
It gathers the material for a single topic.

24
00:01:03.588 --> 00:01:05.983
That material is the context of the task.

25
00:01:06.008 --> 00:01:08.663
When you open codex in a folder, it works from that

26
00:01:08.688 --> 00:01:09.363
folder.

27
00:01:09.388 --> 00:01:13.503
If the folder mixes topics, anything unrelated to the request becomes

28
00:01:13.528 --> 00:01:14.183
noise.

29
00:01:14.208 --> 00:01:17.383
Lucia kept the science fair project in the same folder as

30
00:01:17.408 --> 00:01:18.503
the report cards.

31
00:01:18.528 --> 00:01:21.183
She asked the AI for a summary of the fair and

32
00:01:21.208 --> 00:01:24.348
got a paragraph with a student's grade in the middle.

33
00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:26.923
Once the FAIR folder held only the rules and the list

34
00:01:26.948 --> 00:01:29.428
of groups, the summary talked only about the FAIR.

35
00:01:29.888 --> 00:01:31.728
The AI reads less material.

36
00:01:32.388 --> 00:01:34.508
And you know where every sentence came from.

37
00:01:35.368 --> 00:01:39.148
In the terminal, the tilde symbol stands for your home folder.

38
00:01:39.288 --> 00:01:41.943
It's just a short way of writing the full address, which

39
00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.788
changes from one computer to another.

40
00:01:44.368 --> 00:01:48.403
Denise typed the pwd command, like in module 3, and saw

41
00:01:48.428 --> 00:01:49.168
that whole address.

42
00:01:50.328 --> 00:01:53.983
Inside your home folder, you create a folder called PROJETOS.

43
00:01:54.008 --> 00:01:54.803
Two things go in it.

44
00:01:54.828 --> 00:01:59.103
A config folder, short for configuration, with whatever applies to

45
00:01:59.128 --> 00:02:00.723
any project, like your preferences.

46
00:02:00.748 --> 00:02:05.251
And each project, with its own Entradas folder, the inputs the

47
00:02:05.276 --> 00:02:08.936
AI reads, and its own Cidas folder, the outputs it produces.

48
00:02:09.536 --> 00:02:11.656
Kept apart, you can check one against the other.

49
00:02:12.816 --> 00:02:16.871
Denise prefers short reports with the open items at the end,

50
00:02:16.896 --> 00:02:19.796
both for the console report and for the parents' meeting agenda.

51
00:02:20.176 --> 00:02:22.711
That's why this preference goes into config, just once.

52
00:02:22.736 --> 00:02:27.311
Before you create anything, sketch the tree on paper.

53
00:02:27.336 --> 00:02:30.431
a config folder and a single practice project.

54
00:02:30.456 --> 00:02:32.711
That way you choose the names calmly.

55
00:02:32.736 --> 00:02:37.191
Use short names with no spaces and no accents, because in

56
00:02:37.216 --> 00:02:41.531
the terminal spaces and accents cause trouble in every command.

57
00:02:41.556 --> 00:02:45.311
Lúcia sketched hers on a napkin and in one minute avoided

58
00:02:45.336 --> 00:02:47.431
three folders with similar names.

59
00:02:47.456 --> 00:02:51.491
Then comes the mark direct command with the dash p option,

60
00:02:51.516 --> 00:02:54.291
which creates the folders folders plus any parent folders that are

61
00:02:54.316 --> 00:02:55.071
missing.

62
00:02:55.096 --> 00:02:58.551
If a folder already exists, it stays as it is, and

63
00:02:58.576 --> 00:03:00.131
nothing gets deleted.

64
00:03:00.156 --> 00:03:01.951
Pause the video and do it now.

65
00:03:01.976 --> 00:03:05.519
Sketch the tree, open the terminal from module 3 and paste

66
00:03:05.544 --> 00:03:07.359
the three lines on the screen.

67
00:03:07.384 --> 00:03:11.139
Check that the first ls shows config and meu primeiro projeto

68
00:03:11.164 --> 00:03:14.599
and that the second one shows entradas and saias.

69
00:03:14.624 --> 00:03:18.119
The folder exists, but whoever comes later doesn't know what it's

70
00:03:18.144 --> 00:03:19.199
for.

71
00:03:19.224 --> 00:03:22.879
To write that down, we'll use markdown.

72
00:03:22.904 --> 00:03:27.119
Every one of Lucia's experiment guides has a title, materials and

73
00:03:27.144 --> 00:03:28.044
procedure.

74
00:03:28.224 --> 00:03:33.219
She underlines the titles and puts a dash before each material.

75
00:03:33.244 --> 00:03:35.879
Markdown does the same thing with symbols you type.

76
00:03:35.904 --> 00:03:38.819
A hash sign at the start of a line makes a

77
00:03:38.844 --> 00:03:43.379
title, two hash signs make a subtitle, and a hyphen makes

78
00:03:43.404 --> 00:03:44.019
a list item.

79
00:03:44.044 --> 00:03:47.379
The file is still plain text, so you can read it

80
00:03:47.404 --> 00:03:50.004
even without a special program.

81
00:03:50.244 --> 00:03:54.739
Lúcia moved her bean germination guide to Markdown in 5 minutes,

82
00:03:54.764 --> 00:03:57.384
without learning anything beyond those three marks.

83
00:03:57.524 --> 00:04:01.259
A Markdown file is a text file whose name ends in .md,

84
00:04:01.284 --> 00:04:04.484
and it opens in any editor including the terminal.

85
00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:08.020
The file that explains the project is called README.

86
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.900
The course kit template has five sections.

87
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.620
Purpose, Read First, Organization, How to Check, and Current Status.

88
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.475
You replace each template text with what's true for your project.

89
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:24.755
And if you don't know a field yet, write to be

90
00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:25.640
defined.

91
00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.980
An honest open field is better than template text nobody checked.

92
00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.675
In the Science Fair README, Lucia wrote, under purpose, Organize sign-ups

93
00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.035
for the fair.

94
00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.095
And under how to check, every registered group appears on the

95
00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.180
final list.

96
00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.240
You don't need bold tables or links.

97
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.055
The value comes from clarity.

98
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.775
When Denise took a week off, the colleague covering for her

99
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.015
opened the council project ReadMe and kept the work going without

100
00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:52.820
having to call her.

101
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.800
To edit in the terminal, use Nano.

102
00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.035
It shows the text in the middle and the shortcuts at

103
00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:58.875
the bottom.

104
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.780
The caret symbol means control.

105
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.795
You move around with the arrow keys, save with Ctrl-O, confirm

106
00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.101
the name with Enter, and exit with Ctrl-X.

107
00:05:09.161 --> 00:05:12.216
Then the cat command shows on screen what was saved.

108
00:05:12.241 --> 00:05:14.096
Pause the video and practice.

109
00:05:14.121 --> 00:05:19.096
Go into the Practice Project folder, download the Readme template

110
00:05:19.121 --> 00:05:22.416
with the curl command and open it in Nano.

111
00:05:22.441 --> 00:05:23.921
Heads up!

112
00:05:24.201 --> 00:05:28.056
If you've already written a Readme in that folder, skip the

113
00:05:28.081 --> 00:05:30.961
curl line, because it writes over the file.

114
00:05:31.321 --> 00:05:34.876
Place each template text with something true about your project, or

115
00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:39.436
with to be defined, and describe the Intradas and Cidas folders.

116
00:05:39.461 --> 00:05:42.796
Save, exit and use cat to check that the titles still

117
00:05:42.821 --> 00:05:44.121
have their hash signs.

118
00:05:44.461 --> 00:05:46.616
The Readme talks about one project.

119
00:05:46.641 --> 00:05:50.236
What about things that apply to everything, like your preferences

120
00:05:50.261 --> 00:05:51.316
and decisions?

121
00:05:51.341 --> 00:05:56.181
The school office doesn't write everything in a single notebook.

122
00:05:56.741 --> 00:06:00.976
It has the minutes book, the incidents book, and the procedures

123
00:06:01.001 --> 00:06:01.736
notebook.

124
00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:04.396
And each one answers one question.

125
00:06:04.421 --> 00:06:07.816
In the config folder, four files play that role.

126
00:06:07.841 --> 00:06:11.163
Memoria.md answers how I like the work to come out.

127
00:06:11.883 --> 00:06:15.923
Decisus.md what we chose, when and why.

128
00:06:16.843 --> 00:06:21.303
Dicas.md how it's done, the way that has already worked.

129
00:06:21.403 --> 00:06:25.143
And Falhas.md what went wrong and what the fix was.

130
00:06:25.183 --> 00:06:27.458
Denise was trying to find out why the parents meeting had

131
00:06:27.483 --> 00:06:28.683
moved to Saturday.

132
00:06:28.863 --> 00:06:32.878
The reason was in a 40-page notebook among messages and phone

133
00:06:32.903 --> 00:06:35.783
numbers and it took her half an hour to find it.

134
00:06:36.003 --> 00:06:39.458
A preference is your steady way of working, and it hardly

135
00:06:39.483 --> 00:06:40.683
ever changes.

136
00:06:40.863 --> 00:06:44.163
Lucia prefers exercises with the answer key at the end.

137
00:06:44.483 --> 00:06:45.903
That goes in memoria.

138
00:06:46.183 --> 00:06:48.998
A decision is a choice with a reason, and the reason

139
00:06:49.023 --> 00:06:50.223
can change.

140
00:06:50.283 --> 00:06:53.838
The 8th grade tests have 10 questions because the coordination standardized

141
00:06:53.863 --> 00:06:54.623
them.

142
00:06:54.783 --> 00:06:55.763
That goes in decisus.

143
00:06:56.463 --> 00:06:57.398
With a date.

144
00:06:57.423 --> 00:07:00.678
When a decision changes, don't quietly replace the old line.

145
00:07:00.703 --> 00:07:05.238
In October, the tests went to 12 questions, with 2 on

146
00:07:05.263 --> 00:07:06.403
reading graphs.

147
00:07:06.943 --> 00:07:09.858
Lúcia added a new line and marked the August 1 as

148
00:07:09.883 --> 00:07:10.863
replaced.

149
00:07:11.143 --> 00:07:15.139
The FAUHAS file records the symptom, the confirmed cause, and the

150
00:07:15.164 --> 00:07:15.999
smallest fix.

151
00:07:16.024 --> 00:07:19.499
The minute summary came out empty because the Entradas folder was

152
00:07:19.524 --> 00:07:20.324
empty.

153
00:07:20.764 --> 00:07:23.539
The fix was to check the folder before asking.

154
00:07:23.564 --> 00:07:27.099
You open this file when the problem comes back without pasting

155
00:07:27.124 --> 00:07:28.759
it into every request.

156
00:07:28.784 --> 00:07:31.124
Sometimes a note seems to fit in two files.

157
00:07:32.804 --> 00:07:36.259
Then ask, does it say how I like it, what we

158
00:07:36.284 --> 00:07:39.544
chose, how it's done, or what went wrong?

159
00:07:39.764 --> 00:07:42.144
The first answer that fits decides.

160
00:07:42.464 --> 00:07:44.339
Pause the video and practice.

161
00:07:44.364 --> 00:07:47.679
Go into the config folder and download the 4-kit templates with

162
00:07:47.704 --> 00:07:48.944
the curl command.

163
00:07:49.304 --> 00:07:50.504
One line for each file.

164
00:07:51.324 --> 00:07:54.639
If you've already written any of them, skip its line, because

165
00:07:54.664 --> 00:07:56.404
curl writes over the file.

166
00:07:57.044 --> 00:07:59.379
DLS should show all 4 files.

167
00:07:59.404 --> 00:08:02.759
Then, on paper, say where each of the five made-up notes

168
00:08:02.784 --> 00:08:06.339
from the lesson belongs, like I prefer notices to families in

169
00:08:06.364 --> 00:08:09.519
five lines, or fewer, or report cards in PDF since September

170
00:08:09.544 --> 00:08:12.344
because not every family opens spreadsheets.

171
00:08:12.984 --> 00:08:16.199
Write down the reason for each choice and compare with the

172
00:08:16.224 --> 00:08:16.922
answer key.

173
00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:18.800
And the school system password.

174
00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.060
Which of those four files does it go in?

175
00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:21.740
None of them.

176
00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.675
At the front desk, the keyboard shows which rooms exist.

177
00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.435
And nobody worries about that list being visible.

178
00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.475
The keys are a different story.

179
00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.920
Whoever takes the key opens the room.

180
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.895
A credential works like the key.

181
00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.615
A password, an API key, or a bot token lets whoever

182
00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.275
holds it act on behalf of your account.

183
00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.015
Denise was about to send the team group a screenshot of

184
00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.080
the grading system settings.

185
00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:49.975
Before sending it, she saw the password for the entire coordination

186
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.000
in the corner of the image.

187
00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:54.715
If she had sent it, all 30 people in the group

188
00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:55.720
would have had access.

189
00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:59.255
She covered the password and the team saw what they needed,

190
00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:00.900
with access still limited to the coordination.

191
00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.780
So, where does the credential go?

192
00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:09.195
In a file called .env, it holds variables, a name, an equal

193
00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:12.995
sign, and a value, like a Telegram bot token or a

194
00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.780
database address with the password inside.

195
00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:20.175
Heads up, .env is not encrypted.

196
00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:23.335
Anyone with access to the file can read what's in it,

197
00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:25.795
so it stays only on your machine.

198
00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.495
In module 7, Lucia will create a lookup bot for her

199
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:32.075
class, and its token will live there, and nowhere else.

200
00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.535
To share the project, there's .env.example, with the same names and

201
00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:37.240
made-up values.

202
00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.555
Whoever receives it sees what they need to fill in, but

203
00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.035
gets access to nothing.

204
00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.615
That's how Denise handed the report's project over to the vice

205
00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.715
principal, and the vice principal filled in her own .env.

206
00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:52.195
To help you, the AI almost always only needs to know

207
00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:53.800
that the variable exists.

208
00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.215
Lucia's report wouldn't connect to the school spreadsheet.

209
00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.995
Instead of pasting the .env, she wrote that the database variable

210
00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:04.135
was filled in and asked what to check.

211
00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.935
The help was just as good, and the secret stayed at

212
00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:07.595
home.

213
00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:10.755
If you paste a key by mistake, deleting the message isn't

214
00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:11.140
enough.

215
00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.620
Replace the key on the site that generated it.

216
00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.240
Pause the video and practice.

217
00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:19.251
In the practice project folder, paste the lines on the screen

218
00:10:19.276 --> 00:10:22.531
all at once, without typing them by hand, because of the

219
00:10:22.556 --> 00:10:23.236
quotes.

220
00:10:23.536 --> 00:10:28.711
The printf command writes the two names, with the value preenchalocalmente,

221
00:10:28.736 --> 00:10:33.131
into .env.example Check with cat and with levers.

222
00:10:33.156 --> 00:10:35.291
Dash A.

223
00:10:35.316 --> 00:10:39.931
Don't create the .env now, you'll only need it in module 7.

224
00:10:39.956 --> 00:10:43.891
Now, how do you make sure .env is never saved along with

225
00:10:43.916 --> 00:10:46.216
the project's history, not even by accident?

226
00:10:46.796 --> 00:10:49.476
Each version saved by Git is called a commit.

227
00:10:50.616 --> 00:10:54.071
.gitignore is a text file in the project folder with the

228
00:10:54.096 --> 00:10:55.011
list of what Git should leave out.

229
00:10:55.036 --> 00:11:02.151
That list includes .env, its private variants, and the temporary folders

230
00:11:02.176 --> 00:11:04.391
that programs create on their own.

231
00:11:04.416 --> 00:11:05.876
Everything else goes into the history.

232
00:11:06.596 --> 00:11:10.516
The readme, .env.example and the rest of the work files.

233
00:11:10.696 --> 00:11:15.071
In the reports project, Denise put .env on the list before saving

234
00:11:15.096 --> 00:11:16.136
the first version.

235
00:11:16.476 --> 00:11:17.356
The result?

236
00:11:17.676 --> 00:11:21.296
The file with the grading system password never entered the history.

237
00:11:22.036 --> 00:11:24.631
Git itself only comes in module 6.

238
00:11:24.656 --> 00:11:28.856
For today, it's enough to have .kitignore ready in the folder.

239
00:11:29.096 --> 00:11:31.551
There's a rule about order that trips up a lot of

240
00:11:31.576 --> 00:11:32.336
people.

241
00:11:32.376 --> 00:11:35.876
.kitignore doesn't apply to a file that has already been saved.

242
00:11:35.916 --> 00:11:37.871
It's like the school office courier pouch.

243
00:11:37.896 --> 00:11:41.351
The paper that shouldn't go out comes off the pile before

244
00:11:41.376 --> 00:11:42.751
the pouch is sealed.

245
00:11:42.776 --> 00:11:46.131
Once the pouch has left, crossing the paper off the list

246
00:11:46.156 --> 00:11:47.671
doesn't bring it back.

247
00:11:47.696 --> 00:11:51.411
A colleague of Denise's added .inf to gitignore a week after the

248
00:11:51.436 --> 00:11:54.431
first version, and the file with the password was still there,

249
00:11:54.456 --> 00:11:57.551
in the old version, and if a key leaked, the first

250
00:11:57.576 --> 00:12:00.491
fix is to revoke it at the source, meaning cancel it

251
00:12:00.516 --> 00:12:01.631
on the site that created it.

252
00:12:01.656 --> 00:12:06.371
The API key for one of Lucia's projects showed up in

253
00:12:06.396 --> 00:12:07.731
a version shared with the team.

254
00:12:07.756 --> 00:12:12.411
She cancelled it that same minute, created a new one, and

255
00:12:12.436 --> 00:12:14.291
